    <node id="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia is a type of blood cancer that affects lymphocytes, leading to the overproduction of immature lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing cancer that originates in lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer where the bone marrow produces too many immature lymphocytes called lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing cancer that starts in lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell, and is common in children.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839621</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">B-ALL is a subtype of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia that originates in B-cell lymphocytes and is more common than T-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;B-ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that originates in B-cell lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;B-ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that originates in B-cell lymphocytes and is more common than T-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839621</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T-ALL">
      <data key="d0">T-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">T-ALL is a subtype of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia that originates in T-cell lymphocytes and can cause an enlarged thymus.&lt;SEP&gt;T-ALL is a type of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that originates from T-cell lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;T-ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that originates in T-cell lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;T-ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that originates in T-cell lymphocytes and can cause an enlarged thymus.&lt;SEP&gt;T-ALL is a type of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that originates in T-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839621</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="B-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">B-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">B-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma is a rare subtype of ALL where B-cell lymphoblasts grow primarily in lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;B-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma (B-LL or B-LBL) is a condition where B-cell lymphoblasts grow primarily in the lymph nodes rather than the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839621</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">T-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">T-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma is a subtype of ALL where T-cell lymphoblasts grow primarily in the thymus.&lt;SEP&gt;T-Cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma (T-LL or T-LBL) is a condition where T-cell lymphoblasts grow primarily in the thymus rather than the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric ALL">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric ALL refers to Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia found in infants, children, and young adults, typically under 30 years of age.&lt;SEP&gt;Pediatric ALL refers to acute lymphoblastic leukemia found in infants, children, and young adults.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AYA">
      <data key="d0">AYA</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">AYA stands for Adolescents and Young Adults, defined as those aged 15 to 39 at the time of cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;An adolescent and young adult (AYA) is someone aged 15 to 39 years at the time of initial cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;AYA stands for adolescents and young adults, typically aged 15 to 39 years, who may be treated in pediatric or adult cancer centers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839622</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones where blood cells are produced. It is a soft, spongy or sponge-like tissue found in the center of most bones and is responsible for generating blood cells through the process of hematopoiesis. Bone marrow contains blood-forming stem cells that differentiate into various types of blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets. This tissue plays a critical role in the immune system, as it produces immune cells and is part of the lymphatic system. Bone marrow can be used as a sample for karyotype analysis and is essential in diagnosing conditions such as acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL). Additionally, it is a site where blood-forming stem cells are produced and can be damaged by cancer and its treatments. The function and health of bone marrow are vital to overall blood health and immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
