    <node id="Blood Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Stem Cells are early cells in the bone marrow that give rise to all types of blood cells. These undifferentiated cells have the potential to develop into various types of blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets. Blood stem cells are also described as immature cells capable of differentiating into lymphocytes, myeloid cells, and other blood cell lineages. They serve as the origin of all blood cells and play a critical role in maintaining the body's blood supply. Additionally, Blood Stem Cells are noted as the origin of chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), highlighting their significance in both normal hematopoiesis and disease development. The descriptions consistently emphasize their multipotent nature and their essential function in the formation of the entire blood system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839623</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell involved in the immune system. They play a key role in the body's defense against infections and diseases, and are essential components of the immune response. Lymphocytes include B cells, T cells, and natural killer cells, each with specific functions in identifying and neutralizing pathogens. These cells are central to understanding various immune-related conditions and are involved in the development of lymphomas and other cancers. Specifically, they are the origin of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), and can become cancerous in conditions such as Hodgkin Lymphoma. Lymphocytes are critical to the immune system's ability to fight infections and maintain overall health. Their role in both normal immune function and disease processes highlights their importance in medical research and clinical practice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839624</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoblasts">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoblasts are immature lymphocytes that play a key role in the development of the immune system. They are typically found in the bone marrow and can differentiate into mature lymphocytes. In certain pathological conditions, such as acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), lymphoblasts may be produced in excessive amounts, leading to their accumulation in the blood or bone marrow. This overproduction can result in the crowding out of normal blood cells, causing various clinical manifestations. Lymphoblasts are also associated with other leukemias, where they may multiply uncontrollably and disrupt normal hematopoiesis. In some cases, these immature cells may become cancerous, contributing to the development of leukemia. Additionally, lymphoblasts are sometimes referred to as blasts, particularly in the context of chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), though they are more commonly linked to acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Overall, lymphoblasts represent an early stage in the maturation of lymphocytes and are significant in both normal and pathological hematological processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839624</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoid Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoid Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoid progenitor cells develop into lymphocytes, which are a type of white blood cell involved in immune responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoid progenitor cells develop into lymphoblasts, which mature into lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoid progenitor cells are early-stage cells that develop into lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell affected by ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoid progenitor cells are early-stage cells that develop into lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoid progenitor cells differentiate into lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoid progenitor cells are early cells that develop into lymphoblasts and eventually into lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839624</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloid progenitor cells develop into myeloblasts and other non-lymphoid blood cells, including granulocytes and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells are precursor cells that develop into various types of blood cells, including myeloblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells develop into myeloblasts and other non-lymphoid blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells are early-stage cells that develop into non-lymphoid blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells differentiate into non-lymphoid blood cells, such as granulocytes and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells are early-stage blood cells that develop into myeloblasts and other non-lymphoid blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells are early cells that develop into myeloblasts and other non-lymphoid blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
