    <node id="Blood">
      <data key="d0">Blood</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood is a tissue composed of plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets that performs vital functions in the body. It serves as a liquid tissue that circulates throughout the body, carrying oxygen, nutrients, and waste products. Blood is responsible for transporting oxygen, facilitated by the alveoli, and plays a crucial role in the immune system by fighting infections. Additionally, it helps prevent bleeding through the action of platelets. Blood can also carry cancer cells, which may travel through the bloodstream to form tumors in other parts of the body. Furthermore, blood is used as a sample for various diagnostic purposes, including karyotype analysis, genetic germline testing, and chromosome examination. These functions highlight the essential role of blood in maintaining bodily health and supporting medical diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839625</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Burkitt Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Burkitt Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Burkitt lymphoma (BL) is a type of lymphoma that differs from follicular lymphoma in terms of germinal center structure and cell types.&lt;SEP&gt;Burkitt Lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that is closely related to Burkitt leukemia but has distinct treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Burkitt lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that is similar to mature B-cell ALL but has different treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Burkitt lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that is similar to Burkitt leukemia but has different treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839625</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">White Blood Cells are part of the immune system and play a critical role in helping the body fight infections and diseases. They are a type of blood cell involved in immune responses and are analyzed in various medical tests, including flow cytometry and complete blood counts. White Blood Cells include several subtypes such as granulocytes, monocytes, and lymphocytes, each with specific functions in defending the body against pathogens. These cells are also referred to as WBCs or leukocytes. White Blood Cells help the body combat infections by identifying and neutralizing harmful agents, and their presence and count are important indicators of health and disease. The term "White Blood Cells" is often used interchangeably with "leukocytes," highlighting their shared identity in the context of the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839625</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Killer Cells">
      <data key="d0">Natural Killer Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Natural killer (NK) cells are a type of white blood cell that can kill tumor cells or virus-infected cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural Killer Cells are a type of lymphocyte that can kill tumor cells or virus-infected cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural killer cells destroy tumor cells and virus-infected cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural killer cells are a type of lymphocyte that can kill tumor cells or virus-infected cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839625</data>
