    <node id="Bladder">
      <data key="d0">Bladder</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The bladder is a hollow organ in the urinary system that stores urine until it is expelled from the body. It plays a key role in the urinary process, holding urine before it is released during urination. The bladder can be visualized as a bright spot on PET scans and is often examined through procedures such as cystoscopy. In addition to its primary function of urine storage, the bladder may be affected by certain medical conditions, such as advanced cervical cancer, which can involve nearby organs. The bladder is also the target site for intravesical therapy, a treatment method used in specific urological conditions. Multiple descriptions consistently describe the bladder as a hollow organ responsible for storing urine, with variations in phrasing but no significant contradictions in the core information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839640</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rectum">
      <data key="d0">Rectum</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The rectum is the final section of the large intestine, responsible for storing stool before elimination. It serves as the last part of the large bowel, where feces are stored until they are expelled through the anus. The rectum is also referred to as the last section of the large intestine, leading to the anal canal and playing a key role in the process of defecation. In some medical conditions, such as familial adenomatous polyposis, the rectum may be significantly affected. Additionally, the rectum can be involved in advanced stages of cervical cancer, and it is often removed during procedures like abdominoperineal resection along with the anus. The rectum's primary function is to store feces until they are eliminated from the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
