      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologic Stage">
      <data key="d0">Pathologic Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologic Stage is a classification system used to determine the extent of cancer based on the examination of tissue removed during surgery. It provides more detailed information about the cancer's characteristics, including size, shape, and cell type, which helps in understanding the disease's progression. The pathologic stage is determined after surgery and is considered an accurate description of cancer spread, guiding further treatment decisions. This staging method, also referred to as the surgical stage or pathologic stage (p), involves analyzing tissue samples to assess how far the cancer has advanced. While most descriptions agree that it is determined after surgical removal of tissue, one description mentions that it refers to staging based on findings after treatment has been completed. This may reflect variations in terminology or context, but the core definition remains focused on post-surgical analysis. Overall, Pathologic Stage plays a critical role in evaluating cancer and informing subsequent medical strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
