    <node id="Lymphatic Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphatic vessels are ducts that transport lymph throughout the body, similar to blood vessels.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphatic vessels are a network of thin tubes that carry lymphatic fluid and white blood cells throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphatic vessels are channels that carry lymph throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphatic vessels are thin tubes that carry lymphatic fluid throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic System">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic System</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Lymphatic System is part of the immune system, consisting of a network of tissues, organs, vessels, and nodes that help fight infection and disease. It plays a critical role in defending the body against harmful substances, toxins, and waste by circulating lymph throughout the body. The system includes lymph nodes, vessels, and organs such as the spleen, thymus, and tonsils, which work together to filter and transport lymph.  

The Lymphatic System is also involved in maintaining fluid balance within the body by returning excess interstitial fluid to the bloodstream. It helps remove harmful materials and supports immune function by producing and transporting white blood cells, particularly lymphocytes. Additionally, it is integral to the progression of certain diseases, such as chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), where abnormal lymphocyte activity can affect its normal function.  

This complex system operates alongside the circulatory system, with lymphatic vessels forming a secondary network that complements the blood vessels. Its primary functions include immune defense, waste removal, and fluid regulation, making it essential for overall health and homeostasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
