    </node>
    <node id="Stage">
      <data key="d0">Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage refers to the extent of cancer in the body, determined through the TNM system.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage refers to the extent of cancer in the body, determined by how far it has spread through the colon wall.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage refers to the extent of cancer, determined by imaging and other tests, influencing treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage refers to the extent of cancer growth within the body, measured on a scale from 0 to 4.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Guidelines are evidence-based clinical practice guidelines developed by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network. These guidelines provide comprehensive recommendations for the diagnosis, treatment, and care of various cancers, including but not limited to mouth and head and neck cancers, kidney cancer, early-stage prostate cancer, and lymphomas such as PC-DLBCL, leg type. They are expert-recommended standards for cancer care that help patients understand treatment options and are trusted resources offering evidence-based recommendations. The NCCN Guidelines also include information on treatment options and patient support, making them a valuable tool for both healthcare professionals and patients. Developed by leading cancer centers, these guidelines aim to ensure high-quality, consistent, and up-to-date care across different cancer types, including chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grade">
      <data key="d0">Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade refers to the classification of follicular lymphoma based on the types of cells present, which influences treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade refers to the appearance of tumor cells under a microscope, indicating how abnormal they are.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade describes how abnormal tumor cells look under a microscope, with higher grades indicating faster-growing cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade refers to the classification of a tumor based on how abnormal its cells appear, indicating growth rate and aggressiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade indicates the aggressiveness of cancer, categorized as low grade (slow-growing) or high grade (fast-growing).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Stage">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer stage refers to the extent of cancer in the body, determined by factors such as tumor size and spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer stage describes how far the cancer has grown into the bladder wall, with numbers ranging from 0 to 4.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839654</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Grade">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer grade describes how abnormal tumor cells look under a microscope and is used in staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer grade estimates the rate at which the cancer is growing, either low grade (slow) or high grade (fast).&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer grade refers to how abnormal the cancer cells look under a microscope, with higher grades indicating faster-growing cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
