    </node>
    <node id="General Health Tests">
      <data key="d0">General Health Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">General Health Tests are diagnostic procedures used to evaluate overall health and detect potential issues. These tests encompass a range of medical assessments designed to determine an individual's health status and identify any underlying conditions that may require further attention.  

General Health Tests include medical history reviews, physical exams, and various other assessments that contribute to a comprehensive understanding of a person's health. They are also used in treatment planning, as they provide essential information for healthcare professionals to make informed decisions.  

Additionally, General Health Tests are a set of medical evaluations aimed at assessing overall health and identifying potential issues. These procedures are conducted to evaluate health status and can play a role in the diagnosis and management of various medical conditions, including those related to cancer.  

Overall, General Health Tests serve as a foundational component of healthcare, offering insights into an individual's well-being and helping to guide appropriate medical interventions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle Layer">
      <data key="d0">Muscle Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The muscle layer is a structural component of the stomach wall that can be affected by cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;The muscle layer is responsible for moving food through the stomach.&lt;SEP&gt;Muscle layer is the third layer of the bladder wall, responsible for controlling urine flow.&lt;SEP&gt;The muscle layer is a part of the bladder wall that can be invaded by cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Outer Fatty Layer">
      <data key="d0">Outer Fatty Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Outer fatty layer is the outermost layer of the bladder wall, providing insulation and protection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 is the earliest stage of cancer, often referring to non-invasive or pre-invasive cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 0 is the earliest stage of cancer, indicating no evidence of spread beyond the original site.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 0 pancreatic cancer is confined to the innermost layer of the duct wall.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 0 is a noninvasive stage of breast cancer, typically referring to ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS).&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 0 is the earliest stage of colon cancer, characterized by abnormal cells on the innermost layer of the colon wall.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 0 is the earliest stage of bladder cancer, affecting only the inner lining of the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 0 is a classification of bladder cancer where abnormal cells are present on the inner lining of the bladder but have not invaded deeper layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0a">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0a</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0a is a subgroup of stage 0 where cancer cells grow toward the hollow center of the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0is">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0is</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0is stands for carcinoma in situ, a flat patch of cancer cells that has not invaded deeper layers of the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839655</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3A">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3A</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3A refers to cervical cancer that has grown into the lower third of the vagina.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 3A is a subgroup of stage 3 where cancer may be in the fatty tissue or spread to a single nearby lymph node.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3B">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3B</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3B is a stage of ovarian cancer where cancer is visible on the abdominal lining, with a small area of cancer and possible lymph node involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 3B is a stage of cervical cancer where the tumor has grown into the pelvic wall and/or caused kidney dysfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 3B is a subgroup of stage 3 where cancer has spread to multiple lymph nodes in the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bladder Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the bladder and may be detected through imaging and biopsies. It is characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the bladder, often starting in the urothelial cells. Bladder cancer is a disease that involves the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the bladder and is classified as a type of cancer that affects the urinary tract or urinary system. It can also originate in other parts of the urinary tract and requires a multidisciplinary approach for treatment. The condition is typically treated with various therapies, including chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and targeted therapy, with treatment options depending on its stage and grade. Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the cells of the bladder and is often diagnosed through clinical evaluation and laboratory tests. It is a disease that requires proper diagnosis and treatment to manage effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph Nodes are small structures in the body that are part of the lymphatic system and play a crucial role in filtering lymph, removing harmful substances, and supporting the immune system. These small, bean-shaped structures contain immune cells that help fight infections and diseases. Lymph nodes can become enlarged in response to infections, cancer, or other conditions, making them an important indicator during physical exams. They are often examined for signs of cancer spread, as they can be affected by metastasis from various types of cancer, including basal cell skin cancer, esophageal cancer, ovarian cancer, stomach cancer, and bladder cancer. In some cases, lymph nodes may contain cancer cells, which can influence cancer staging and treatment decisions. During surgery, affected lymph nodes may be removed, especially in cases where cancer has spread. Lymph nodes are also targeted during radiation therapy, particularly in areas such as the pelvis and groin, when treating cancers like anal cancer. Overall, lymph nodes serve as critical components of the immune system and are frequently involved in the progression and diagnosis of various malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4A">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4A</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4A is a stage of cervical cancer where the tumor has spread to nearby organs such as the bladder or rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4A is a stage of cervical cancer where cancer has spread to nearby organs such as the bladder or rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4A is a subgroup of stage 4 where cancer has invaded the pelvic or abdominal wall or spread to distant lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839656</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4B">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4B</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4B refers to metastatic cervical cancer that has spread to distant sites such as the liver, lungs, or bone.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4B is a subgroup of stage 4 where cancer has spread to distant lymph nodes and organs such as the bones, liver, or lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839657</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Low Grade (LG)">
      <data key="d0">Low Grade (LG)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Low grade (LG) refers to slow-growing cancer cells that are more similar to normal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Low grade refers to cancer cells that resemble normal cells and are slow-growing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839657</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High Grade (HG)">
      <data key="d0">High Grade (HG)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">High grade (HG) refers to fast-growing cancer cells that look very different from normal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;High grade refers to cancer cells that look very different from normal cells and are fast-growing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839657</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Cells">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial cells are stretchy cells that line the inside of the bladder and other parts of the urinary tract.&lt;SEP&gt;Urothelial cells are the cells that line the inner surface of the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Urothelial cells are the cells that line the inside of the bladder and are the most common origin of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839657</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 is a classification of various types of cancer that indicates a more advanced stage than Stage 1. In non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), Stage 2 refers to a locally advanced stage where the cancer has grown into lung tissue and may have spread to nearby lymph nodes. For rectal cancer, Stage 2 describes a condition where the tumor has grown through the rectal wall and possibly into nearby tissues. In pancreatic cancer, Stage 2 includes tumors larger than 4 centimeters or cancer that has spread to 1 to 3 nearby lymph nodes. In breast cancer, Stage 2 is an invasive stage where the cancer may have spread to nearby lymph nodes. For colon cancer, Stage 2 is characterized by a tumor that has grown beyond the fourth layer of the colon wall but has not spread to lymph nodes or other areas. In bladder cancer, Stage 2 refers to a stage where the cancer has grown into the muscle layer but not the fatty layer, and it is also described as a classification where the tumor has invaded the muscle layer of the bladder wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1 is a classification of various types of cancer that indicate early-stage disease. In non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), Stage 1 refers to a condition where the cancer has grown from the airway into the lung tissue but has not spread to lymph nodes. In breast cancer, Stage 1 describes an early invasive stage where the cancer has grown outside the ducts but has not yet spread to lymph nodes. For rectal cancer, Stage 1 involves a tumor that has grown into the second or third layer of the rectal wall. In colon cancer, Stage 1 is characterized by a tumor that has grown into the second or third layer of the colon wall without spreading to lymph nodes or other areas. For pancreatic cancer, Stage 1 involves a tumor that is 4 centimeters or smaller and has not spread outside the pancreas. In bladder cancer, Stage 1 indicates that the cancer has grown through the inner lining and into the connective tissue layer of the bladder wall. Another description of Stage 1 in bladder cancer specifies that a tumor has formed and has invaded the connective tissue layer of the bladder wall. These descriptions highlight that while Stage 1 generally represents an early and localized form of cancer, the specific criteria vary depending on the type of cancer being assessed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839658</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Abdomen">
      <data key="d0">Abdomen</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The abdomen is the lower part of the torso, containing organs such as the stomach, liver, and ovaries.&lt;SEP&gt;The abdomen is the area of the body between the chest and the pelvis, containing many internal organs.&lt;SEP&gt;The abdomen is the area of the body containing the stomach, intestines, and other organs, often scanned during imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdomen is the area of the body between the chest and pelvis, containing various organs.&lt;SEP&gt;The abdomen is the area of the body between the chest and the pelvis, often examined in cancer imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3 is a classification of various types of cancer that indicates further progression of the disease. In non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), Stage 3 refers to a locally advanced stage where cancer has grown into lung tissue and may have spread to more distant lymph nodes. In rectal cancer, Stage 3 describes a condition where the cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes. For pancreatic cancer, Stage 3 involves cancer that has grown into major arteries or spread to four or more lymph nodes. In breast cancer, Stage 3 is an advanced invasive stage where cancer has spread to multiple lymph nodes or nearby tissues. In colon cancer, Stage 3 is characterized by a tumor that has spread to nearby lymph nodes or has tumor deposits in the fat around the colon. In cervical cancer, Stage 3 refers to a stage where cancer has spread to the pelvic wall or causes kidney problems. In bladder cancer, Stage 3 is a stage where the cancer has grown through the bladder wall and into the fatty layer, or where the tumor has invaded the fat layer around the bladder and may involve nearby lymph nodes. Each of these descriptions highlights the progression of cancer in different organs, emphasizing the involvement of surrounding tissues, lymph nodes, or other adjacent structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 is a metastatic stage of various types of cancer, indicating advanced disease where cancer has spread beyond the original site to other organs or distant parts of the body. Stage 4 is a classification of cancer that indicates advanced or metastatic disease, often involving the spread to areas such as the lungs, liver, or internal organs. In the context of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), Stage 4 refers to a metastatic stage where cancer has spread to other organs or the tissue lining around the lungs. For ovarian cancer, Stage 4 is an advanced stage where cancer has spread to distant parts of the body, including the lungs or internal organs. In pancreatic cancer, Stage 4 is metastatic, meaning it has spread far from the pancreas, often to the liver, lungs, or abdominal lining. For breast cancer, Stage 4 is a metastatic stage where cancer has spread to distant parts of the body. In colon cancer, Stage 4 is a stage where the cancer has spread to areas outside the colon, commonly the liver and lungs. In bladder cancer, Stage 4 is the most advanced stage, where cancer has grown through the bladder wall and may have spread to distant parts of the body. Additionally, Stage 4 is a classification of bladder cancer where the cancer has spread beyond the bladder to distant parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bones">
      <data key="d0">Bones</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Bones are the rigid structures of the skeletal system, which can be affected by metastatic prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bones are rigid structures that form the skeleton and provide support and protection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839659</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Gland">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The prostate gland is an important part of the male reproductive system.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate gland is a small walnut-shaped organ in the male reproductive system that produces seminal fluid.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate gland is a male reproductive organ that produces fluid for semen and is the site of prostate cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate gland is a male reproductive organ located near the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Standard-of-Care Approaches">
      <data key="d0">Standard-of-Care Approaches</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard-of-care approaches are the conventional treatments used in medical practice for a given condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard-of-care approaches are established medical treatments considered the best available for a given condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard-of-care approaches refer to the typical treatments used for breast cancer, such as surgery, chemotherapy, and radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839660</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastasis refers to the spread of cancer from its original site to other parts of the body. This process involves cancer cells moving from the primary tumor to distant locations, where they can form secondary tumors. Metastasis is typically facilitated through the bloodstream or lymphatic system. Common sites for metastasis include lymph nodes, lungs, liver, and bones. The term describes both the movement of cancer cells and the formation of new tumors in these locations. Metastasis is a critical factor in the progression of cancer and significantly impacts prognosis and treatment options. It is a key characteristic that distinguishes malignant tumors from benign ones. The spread of cancer through metastasis can occur at various stages of the disease and is a major concern in oncology. Understanding metastasis is essential for developing effective cancer therapies and improving patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prognostic Stages">
      <data key="d0">Prognostic Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Prognostic stages are classifications used to assess the likely course and outcome of a disease, divided into clinical and pathologic stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer staging is a process used to determine the extent of cancer and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is the process of determining how advanced a cancer is, often performed before and after surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is the process of determining how far a cancer has spread in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is the process of determining how far cancer has spread in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is a system used to classify the extent of cancer in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Staging is the process of determining how far cancer has spread in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is the process of determining how far the cancer has spread, with stages I through III indicating nonmetastatic disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Plan">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment Plan is a structured approach to managing a patient's medical condition, based on diagnostic findings, test results, and medical evaluation. It serves as a personalized strategy developed by healthcare providers or a care team to address the specific needs and condition of the patient. The plan typically includes a set of medical interventions designed to manage and treat the patient's condition, with goals, potential side effects, and possible changes outlined. Treatment plans are influenced by various factors, including performance status, risk assessment, and the specific characteristics of the patient's illness. In the context of cancer, a treatment plan outlines the chosen therapies and their expected outcomes, often developed after confirming the diagnosis. It may also involve a strategy for managing cancer, considering staging, grading, and other relevant factors. For patients with primary CNS lymphoma, a treatment plan is specifically developed to guide medical interventions. Overall, a treatment plan is a comprehensive and dynamic document that reflects the collaborative efforts of the care team to provide effective and individualized care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor is an abnormal mass of tissue that results from uncontrolled cell growth and can be benign or malignant. It is characterized by an abnormal accumulation of cells that may grow into nearby structures and can be measured in centimeters or millimeters. Tumors can be treated through various methods, including surgery, and are often identified through imaging tests or biopsy for diagnosis. Some tumors are associated with cancer, while others are non-cancerous. The term "Tis" is used to indicate carcinoma in situ, a type of tumor that has not yet spread beyond its original location. In certain contexts, such as cervical cancer, tumors are evaluated to determine the stage of the disease. Additionally, tumors may be analyzed for molecular features, biomarkers, or other characteristics to guide treatment decisions. Tumor testing and examination after surgery are common practices to understand the nature and behavior of the mass. In some cases, tumors refer specifically to the primary cancerous growth, with terms like "T" describing their size and depth. Overall, tumors represent a broad category of abnormal tissue growths that can vary in origin, behavior, and clinical significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvis">
      <data key="d0">Pelvis</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The pelvis is the lower part of the torso, containing various organs such as the bladder, reproductive organs, and digestive structures. It is also described as a basin-shaped structure that supports the spinal column and protects abdominal organs. The pelvis includes the hip bones and the area around the anus, and it plays a role in the assessment of conditions like anal cancer during imaging tests. Additionally, it contains pelvic lymph nodes and may be affected by radiation treatment, which can lead to weakened bones. The pelvis is often involved in cancer staging and metastasis, particularly in cases related to anal cancer. It is a region of the body that is frequently scanned in medical imaging to evaluate its structures and any potential abnormalities. The term "pelvic organs" is commonly used to refer to the collection of organs located within this anatomical region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Drug Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Drug Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer drug therapy is a treatment administered before surgery, which can affect the staging classification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Throat Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Throat cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the oropharynx, including the tonsils, soft palate, and base of the tongue.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer refers to cancer that develops in the throat, including the pharynx and oropharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer refers to malignancies affecting the oropharynx and is diagnosed through various tests and procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat Cancer is a type of cancer that can be linked to HPV infection and is assessed using p16 testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer is a type of cancer that can spread to nearby lymph nodes and is staged using the TNM system.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer, also known as oropharyngeal cancer, is a type of cancer that develops in the throat area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The head and neck region contains a network of lymph nodes and is a common site for cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck refers to the anatomical region where throat cancer can occur and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer that starts in lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="B-Cell ALL">
      <data key="d0">B-Cell ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell ALL or B-ALL is a type of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that starts in B cells.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell ALL (B-ALL) is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that originates in B-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T-Cell ALL">
      <data key="d0">T-Cell ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell ALL or T-ALL is a type of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that starts in T cells.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell ALL (T-ALL) is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that originates in T-cell lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="p16- (HPV-) Cancer">
      <data key="d0">p16- (HPV-) Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">p16- (HPV-) cancer is a type of throat cancer not caused by human papillomavirus (HPV).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="p16-Positive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">p16-Positive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">disease</data>
      <data key="d2">p16-positive cancer is a type of throat cancer associated with human papillomavirus (HPV).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Locoregional or Locally Advanced">
      <data key="d0">Locoregional or Locally Advanced</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Locoregional or locally advanced refers to cancer that has spread to nearby tissues, organs, or lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Locoregional or locally advanced refers to a tumor that has spread to nearby tissues, organs, or lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Moderately or Very Advanced Disease">
      <data key="d0">Moderately or Very Advanced Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Moderately or very advanced disease refers to cancer that has spread to areas such as the jaw, sinuses, or skull.&lt;SEP&gt;Moderately or very advanced disease refers to cancer that has spread to multiple areas in the head and neck.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage I">
      <data key="d0">Stage I</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage I is an early stage of cancer with limited spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lungs">
      <data key="d0">Lungs</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The Lungs are a pair of organs in the chest responsible for gas exchange, moving oxygen into the blood and carbon dioxide out. They are the primary organs of the respiratory system, playing a critical role in breathing and the exchange of gases. The Lungs are examined during physical exams and imaging tests, as they can be affected by various conditions, including cancer.  

The Lungs are part of the respiratory system and can be affected by cancer spread, including from rectal, breast, colon, cervical, stomach, and anal cancers. They are a common site for metastasis in several types of cancer, such as breast and colon cancer, and may also be impacted by metastatic anal cancer. Additionally, the Lungs are a distant organ that may be affected by metastatic disease, making them an important area of focus in cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839662</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage II">
      <data key="d0">Stage II</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage II is a more advanced stage of cancer with some spread to nearby tissues or lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage III">
      <data key="d0">Stage III</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage III is a more advanced stage of cancer with significant spread to nearby tissues or lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stage IV">
      <data key="d0">Stage IV</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage IV is the most advanced stage of cancer, often involving distant metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T1">
      <data key="d0">T1</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">T1 is a staging category indicating a small tumor with limited depth of invasion.&lt;SEP&gt;T1 is a stage in the TNM system describing a tumor that is 2 cm or smaller.&lt;SEP&gt;T1 indicates a tumor size of 2 cm or less.&lt;SEP&gt;T1 is a stage where the tumor is confined to the nasopharynx or has grown into the oropharynx and/or nasal cavity.&lt;SEP&gt;T1 indicates a tumor that is 8 cm or smaller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T2">
      <data key="d0">T2</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">T2 is a staging category indicating a larger tumor or deeper invasion than T1.&lt;SEP&gt;T2 is a stage in the TNM system describing a tumor that is between 2 and 4 cm.&lt;SEP&gt;T2 indicates a tumor size of 1 cm to 5 cm.&lt;SEP&gt;T2 is a stage where the tumor has grown into the parapharyngeal space and/or nearby soft tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;T2 indicates a tumor larger than 8 cm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T3">
      <data key="d0">T3</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">T3 is a staging category indicating a larger tumor with significant depth of invasion.&lt;SEP&gt;T3 is a stage in the TNM system describing a tumor that is larger than 4 cm or has grown into the epiglottis.&lt;SEP&gt;T3 indicates a tumor larger than 5 cm.&lt;SEP&gt;T3 indicates more than one tumor in the primary bone site.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T4a">
      <data key="d0">T4a</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">T4a is a staging category indicating moderately advanced local disease with tumor invasion into nearby structures.&lt;SEP&gt;T4a is a stage in the TNM system describing moderately advanced local disease, where the tumor invades specific structures.&lt;SEP&gt;T4a indicates a tumor involving SIJ and extending into the spine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="T4b">
      <data key="d0">T4b</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">T4b is a staging category indicating very advanced local disease with extensive tumor invasion.&lt;SEP&gt;T4b is a stage in the TNM system describing very advanced local disease, where the tumor invades deeper structures or encases major blood vessels.&lt;SEP&gt;T4b indicates a tumor growing into major blood vessels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839663</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Carotid Artery">
      <data key="d0">Carotid Artery</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The carotid artery is a major blood vessel in the neck that supplies blood to the brain and is mentioned as a possible location for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The carotid artery is a major blood vessel in the neck that can be affected by advanced throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
