    <node id="TNM System">
      <data key="d0">TNM System</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM System is a classification and staging system used in oncology to describe the extent of cancer. It is based on three key components: T, N, and M, which stand for tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis, respectively. The system provides a standardized approach to evaluating the progression of various types of cancer. It is widely utilized across different cancer types, including mouth cancer, throat cancer, breast cancer, colon cancer, bone cancers, and anal cancer. Each component of the TNM system helps clinicians assess the severity and spread of the disease, enabling more accurate diagnosis and treatment planning. The TNM system is employed by cancer experts as a consistent framework for describing the characteristics of tumors and their progression. While the specific application may vary depending on the cancer type, the core principles of tumor size, lymph node involvement, and distant metastasis remain consistent across its use cases. The system serves as an essential tool in both clinical practice and cancer research.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
