    <node id="AML">
      <data key="d0">AML</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of blood cancer that affects the bone marrow and blood. It is characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal white blood cells, specifically myeloid progenitor cells, which leads to the production of abnormal blasts. AML is an acute form of leukemia, meaning it progresses quickly and requires prompt treatment. The disease originates in the stem cells of the bone marrow and can lead to the buildup of these abnormal cells, interfering with the normal function of the blood. Diagnosis often involves bone marrow testing to confirm the presence of the condition. AML is typically treated with chemotherapy, though other therapies may also be used depending on the patient’s condition. It is classified as a type of cancer that affects both the blood and bone marrow, and it is also known by its full name, Acute Myeloid Leukemia. The term AML is commonly used as an abbreviation for this condition, which is a type of leukemia and a form of blood cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839667</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
