    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL is a subtype of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia with genetic features similar to BCR::ABL1-positive leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839671</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a subtype of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia characterized by the presence of the BCR::ABL1 gene fusion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839671</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Infant ALL">
      <data key="d0">Infant ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Infant ALL is a form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that occurs in children under 12 months of age.&lt;SEP&gt;Infant ALL refers to acute lymphoblastic leukemia diagnosed in children under 12 months of age, requiring specialized treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839671</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Rectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the rectum, often starting in polyps such as adenomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the rectum and can originate from polyps or lesions.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the rectum, the last part of the large intestine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839671</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurrence refers to the return of cancer after initial treatment, often indicating the need for further intervention. This phenomenon can occur in the original area where the cancer first developed or in other parts of the body. Recurrence is commonly observed after a period of remission or when the disease reappears following a period of being cancer-free. It may require additional evaluation and treatment to manage effectively. In some cases, recurrence specifically relates to the return of basal cell skin cancer. Certain treatments, such as intravesical BCG, are designed to prevent recurrence. Overall, recurrence signifies the reappearance of cancer after it has been treated, highlighting the importance of ongoing monitoring and potential follow-up care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839671</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Polyps are non-cancerous growths that form on the inner lining of the colon and rectum, with some types potentially becoming cancerous.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are abnormal growths on the lining of the rectum that can become cancerous.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are non-cancerous growths that can develop in the colon or rectum and may become cancerous over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are non-cancerous growths in the colon or rectum that can develop into cancer over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are abnormal growths in the colon that can become cancerous if not removed.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are abnormal growths on the inner lining of the colon that can become cancerous over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839671</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Kidneys">
      <data key="d0">Kidneys</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidneys are organs responsible for filtering waste from the blood and producing urine. They are paired organs located behind other abdominal organs and play a critical role in maintaining homeostasis by removing excess water and waste from the body. The kidneys also regulate fluid balance and produce hormones, contributing to overall physiological stability.  

In addition to their primary function of filtering blood, the kidneys can be affected by certain medications, such as high-dose methotrexate and cisplatin, which may require monitoring during treatment. Their function is also relevant in medical procedures, such as imaging tests involving contrast material and ureteroscopy, where kidney health is a key consideration.  

Abnormalities in kidney function may be indicated by irregular results in metabolic panel tests. Furthermore, the kidneys are assessed when determining the viability of systemic therapy for conditions like bladder cancer, highlighting their importance in clinical decision-making. Overall, the kidneys are vital organs essential for maintaining bodily equilibrium and supporting various diagnostic and therapeutic processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839671</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperplastic Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Hyperplastic Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperplastic polyps are a type of polyp that rarely turn into cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperplastic polyps are non-cancerous growths in the colon or rectum that rarely turn into cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperplastic Polyps are non-cancerous growths in the colon that rarely turn into cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperplastic polyps are non-cancerous growths in the colon that may have a low risk of becoming cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839672</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urethra">
      <data key="d0">Urethra</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The urethra is a tube that carries urine from the bladder to the outside of the body. It serves as a passageway for urine and, in males, also for semen. The urethra is surrounded by the prostate, which wraps around it. This anatomical structure is examined during procedures such as cystoscopy and is involved in medical treatments like intravesical therapy for medication delivery. In some contexts, the urethra is also utilized during transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT) procedures. The primary function of the urethra is to transport urine from the bladder out of the body, though its role can extend depending on the physiological or medical context. The descriptions consistently describe the urethra as a tubular structure with a primary function related to urine excretion, with additional details about its location and involvement in medical procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839672</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory polyps are a type of polyp that rarely turn into cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory polyps are non-cancerous growths that occur in the colon or rectum due to inflammation.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory Polyps are non-cancerous growths in the colon that rarely become cancerous.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory polyps are non-cancerous growths in the colon that are often associated with chronic inflammation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
