    <node id="Adenoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenoma</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenoma is a type of polyp that can develop into invasive colon cancer if left untreated. It is commonly associated with the gastrointestinal tract, particularly the colon, and is characterized by its potential to become cancerous over time. Adenomas found during colonoscopy may require more frequent follow-up testing if they are considered concerning or high-risk.  

Adenoma is also described as a common type of polyp that can become invasive rectal cancer over time. This highlights the similar nature of adenomas in different parts of the digestive system, where they may progress to malignancy if not monitored or treated.  

In addition to its association with the colon and rectum, adenoma is recognized as a type of growth that has the potential to become cancerous. It is sometimes referred to as an adrenocortical adenoma, which is the most common type of adrenal tumor and is typically noncancerous. This distinction indicates that while the term "adenoma" is often used in the context of the gastrointestinal tract, it can also refer to tumors in other organs, such as the adrenal glands.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839674</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Large Intestine">
      <data key="d0">Large Intestine</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The large intestine is part of the digestive system, including the colon and rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;The large intestine is responsible for absorbing water and forming waste for elimination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839674</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer that originates in glandular cells, which are specialized cells found in various organs and tissues throughout the body. It is most commonly associated with the lungs, where it is classified as a subtype of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) and typically begins in the mucus-producing cells of the alveoli. Adenocarcinoma is also recognized as the most common type of colon cancer, originating from adenomas, and as the most common form of rectal cancer, which similarly starts in an adenoma. In the stomach, it is the most common type of cancer, developing from mucus-secreting cells. Adenocarcinoma can also occur in the lower esophagus, where it originates in glandular cells, and in the cervix, where it begins in the glandular cells of the endocervix. Additionally, it may arise in the bladder, where it is considered a rare form of non-urothelial bladder cancer that originates in glandular cells. Despite its association with multiple organs, adenocarcinoma is consistently defined by its origin in glandular tissue, distinguishing it from other types of cancer that arise from different cell types. The term "adenocarcinoma" is used across various medical contexts to describe this specific type of malignancy, highlighting its prevalence and varied locations within the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839674</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Anus">
      <data key="d0">Anus</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The anus is the opening at the end of the digestive tract through which stool is expelled.&lt;SEP&gt;The anus is the opening through which stool exits the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The anus is the opening at the end of the digestive tract through which stool exits the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The anus is the opening through which stool exits the body, located at the end of the large bowel.&lt;SEP&gt;The anus is the primary site of anal cancer and may be removed during abdominoperineal resection.&lt;SEP&gt;The opening at the end of the large bowel through which stool exits the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839674</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lumbar Puncture">
      <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Lumbar Puncture is a medical procedure used to collect cerebrospinal fluid for diagnostic purposes. The procedure involves inserting a needle into the lower back to access the spinal fluid, which is then analyzed for various conditions. Lumbar puncture (LP) is commonly performed to check for abnormalities such as infections, bleeding, or the presence of cancer cells in the central nervous system. It is also used to detect the spread of certain cancers, such as lymphoma, by examining the cerebrospinal fluid. The primary goal of the procedure is to obtain a sample of the fluid for laboratory testing, which can provide critical information about a patient's neurological health. While the descriptions vary slightly in wording, they all consistently describe the same procedure and its purpose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839674</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Tests">
      <data key="d0">Heart Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Heart Tests are medical procedures used to evaluate the function and health of the heart. These tests are designed to assess how well the heart is working and can detect heart-related issues. They are commonly used to monitor treatment side effects, particularly in patients undergoing cancer treatments, where heart function may be affected. Heart Tests also play an important role in evaluating cardiac health before treatment begins. In addition to assessing heart function, these procedures help identify potential issues and provide valuable information for diagnosis and management. The primary goal of Heart Tests is to ensure that the heart is functioning properly and to guide appropriate medical interventions. Some descriptions specifically mention their relevance during chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) treatment, highlighting their importance in monitoring heart health throughout various medical processes. Overall, Heart Tests are essential tools in cardiology and are used across different clinical scenarios to maintain and evaluate heart health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839674</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rectal Wall">
      <data key="d0">Rectal Wall</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The rectal wall is composed of layers of tissue that can be affected by cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The rectal wall is the structure that contains the rectum, and tumors may invade its layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
