    </node>
    <node id="Colonoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Colonoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonoscopy is a medical procedure that uses a scope to examine the large bowel. It is primarily used to examine the entire colon and detect polyps or cancer, and it may also involve the removal of polyps through a minor surgical technique called polypectomy. Colonoscopy allows doctors to examine the inside of the colon and rectum, and it is often recommended for follow-up intervals depending on the patient's condition. In some cases, it is specifically used for stage 1 colon cancer patients one year after surgery, with follow-up schedules determined by the results. Additionally, colonoscopy is employed to detect new cancer growth and to assess the effectiveness of previous treatments. The procedure plays a critical role in both diagnostic and preventive healthcare, particularly in early detection and management of colorectal diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839679</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Testicles">
      <data key="d0">Testicles</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Testicles are the male reproductive organs responsible for producing sperm and testosterone. They play a crucial role in the male reproductive system and are essential for fertility and hormone regulation. Testicles can be affected by various types of cancer, including leukemia and lymphoma, with cancer cells sometimes spreading to these areas. Additionally, cancer treatments, such as chemotherapy or radiation, may damage the testicles, potentially affecting sperm production. In some cases, the testicles may be examined for signs of lymphoma or other malignancies. The testicles are also vulnerable to conditions that can impact their function, making them an important focus in both medical diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839679</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic cancer has spread to distant parts of the body, such as the liver, lungs, and brain.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond its original location to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic Cancer is a stage of cervical cancer that has spread to distant parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer is cancer that has spread from its original site to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread from its original location to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread from its original site to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839679</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Local Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Local Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Local therapies are treatments aimed at specific areas of the body, such as liver and lung tumors in metastatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839679</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Long-Term Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Long-Term Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Long-term side effects are health issues that persist after cancer treatment has ended.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839679</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Healthy Habits">
      <data key="d0">Healthy Habits</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Healthy habits include lifestyle choices that support overall well-being and recovery after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Healthy habits refer to lifestyle choices that support overall well-being and recovery after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839679</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Family Health History">
      <data key="d0">Family Health History</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Family health history refers to the genetic and medical information of relatives, which can indicate predispositions to certain diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history is information about the medical conditions of a person's relatives, which can help assess cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history refers to the pattern of diseases and conditions within a family, which can help assess genetic risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history refers to the medical history of biological family members, which can indicate cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history refers to the medical history of a person's relatives, which can indicate hereditary risks for diseases like colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839679</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chemistry Profile">
      <data key="d0">Chemistry Profile</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemistry profile is a set of blood tests that evaluate organ function and metabolic status.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemistry profile is a group of tests evaluating the function of organs such as the kidneys and liver.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemistry profile, also known as a comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP), assesses organ function.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemistry profile is a group of blood tests that provide information about the body's chemical balance and organ function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839680</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CEA Blood Test">
      <data key="d0">CEA Blood Test</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">CEA blood test measures carcinoembryonic antigen levels, which may indicate colorectal cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;The CEA blood test measures carcinoembryonic antigen levels, which can indicate colon cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839680</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis">
      <data key="d0">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP) is a genetic condition linked to an elevated risk of adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an inherited condition causing numerous polyps in the colon, which can lead to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Familial adenomatous polyposis is an inherited condition characterized by the development of numerous polyps in the colon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839680</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ctDNA">
      <data key="d0">ctDNA</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) is DNA released by cancer cells into the bloodstream, used for monitoring cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;ctDNA refers to circulating tumor DNA, which is used in testing for early-stage colon cancer and can detect microscopic disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839680</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Colon">
      <data key="d0">Colon</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The colon is a part of the large intestine responsible for absorbing water and forming stool.&lt;SEP&gt;The Colon is the largest part of the large intestine, responsible for absorbing water from food and forming stool.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon is a part of the digestive system where colon cancer typically originates.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon is a part of the large intestine, where polyps and cancer can develop.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon is part of the large intestine and is responsible for absorbing water and forming stool.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon is a part of the large intestine responsible for absorbing water and forming feces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839680</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Stable">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Stable</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite stable refers to a genetic characteristic of cancer cells that indicates stability in DNA replication.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite stable (MSS) refers to tumors that do not show microsatellite instability, indicating a lack of genetic instability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839680</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Proficient">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Proficient</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch repair proficient (pMMR) refers to tumors that have normal mismatch repair function, indicating no deficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839681</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Sample">
      <data key="d0">Blood Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood sample is used in various tests to analyze genetic changes in AML cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood sample is a small amount of blood taken from a patient for diagnostic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood sample is a type of biological sample used in liquid biopsies to detect cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood sample is a small amount of blood taken from a patient for diagnostic or genetic testing purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Sample is a sample collected from a patient's blood for laboratory testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839681</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Saliva Sample">
      <data key="d0">Saliva Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A saliva sample is a biological sample collected from a patient for genetic testing purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839681</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CEA">
      <data key="d0">CEA</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">CEA (Carcinoembryonic Antigen) is a protein found in blood that may indicate colorectal cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;CEA (Carcinoembryonic Antigen) is a protein found in blood that can indicate the presence of colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839681</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Blood Count">
      <data key="d0">Complete Blood Count</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Complete Blood Count is a blood test that measures different components of blood. It is commonly used to evaluate the overall health of a person and to detect a variety of medical conditions. The test specifically measures the levels of red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets in the blood. These components are essential for various bodily functions, including oxygen transport, immune response, and clotting. A complete blood count provides information about the types and numbers of cells in the blood, which helps healthcare professionals diagnose and monitor a wide range of health issues. The term Complete Blood Count (CBC) is often used interchangeably with the full name, and it refers to the same diagnostic procedure. The test is widely regarded as a standard and routine medical examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839681</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Survivorship">
      <data key="d0">Survivorship</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Survivorship refers to the care and support provided to individuals who have completed cancer treatment and are managing long-term effects. It encompasses the experience of those living with or beyond their diagnosis, focusing on both physical and emotional well-being. Survivorship includes regular follow-up and monitoring for recurrence, as well as the management of long-term health effects and quality of life. This phase of life extends beyond surveillance testing and involves ongoing care and support for individuals in remission. Survivorship is also defined as the care and management of individuals who have completed cancer treatment, emphasizing the need for continued attention to their health and well-being. It highlights the unique challenges faced by survivors as they transition from active treatment to post-treatment life. The concept of survivorship covers both the medical and psychosocial aspects of care, ensuring that survivors receive comprehensive support throughout their journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839681</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A cervical biopsy involves removing small tissue samples from the cervix to diagnose cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A cervical biopsy involves removing tissue samples from the cervix for testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839681</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lynch Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Lynch Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lynch Syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of various cancers, including ovarian, colorectal, pancreatic, and bladder cancers. It is an inherited condition caused by mutations in mismatch repair (MMR) genes, which play a critical role in correcting errors during DNA replication. These genetic mutations lead to an increased susceptibility to several types of cancer, with colorectal cancer being one of the most commonly associated malignancies. Lynch Syndrome is also known to elevate the risk of developing adrenal tumors, although this is less frequently emphasized in descriptions. The condition is characterized by its hereditary nature, meaning it can be passed from one generation to the next. While some descriptions highlight specific cancers such as ovarian or pancreatic, others refer more generally to "certain cancers" or "various cancers," indicating a broad range of potential malignancies linked to the syndrome. Overall, Lynch Syndrome is a genetic disorder with significant implications for cancer risk, particularly for individuals with a family history of the condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839682</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cone Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cone Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A cone biopsy is a procedure that removes a cone-shaped portion of the cervix for diagnostic or treatment purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;A cone biopsy removes a cone-shaped portion of the cervix for diagnostic or therapeutic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone Biopsy is a procedure to remove a cone-shaped piece of tissue from the cervix for diagnostic or therapeutic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone biopsy is a procedure where a cone-shaped piece of tissue is removed from the cervix for examination, often used to detect cancer or pre-cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839682</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counselor">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counselor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic Counselor is a healthcare professional who provides guidance and information about genetic conditions and testing. The role involves helping individuals understand genetic risks, interpret test results, and make informed decisions about their health. Genetic counselors are trained experts who offer support and education regarding genetic testing, inherited conditions, and the implications of test outcomes. They also assist patients in understanding family history and how it may impact treatment decisions. In addition to providing guidance on genetic risks and testing, they may order tests for inherited cancer syndromes. Their work includes offering support throughout the genetic testing process and explaining the potential consequences of test results. Genetic counselors play a key role in bridging the gap between complex genetic information and patient understanding, ensuring that individuals are well-informed about their options and the possible outcomes of genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839682</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="FIGO System">
      <data key="d0">FIGO System</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The FIGO system is a classification method used to stage cervical cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The FIGO system is used to stage cervical cancer based on its extent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839682</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d0">Trachelectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Trachelectomy is a surgery to remove the cervix while preserving the uterus and ovaries.&lt;SEP&gt;Trachelectomy is a fertility-sparing surgery that removes the cervix but preserves the uterus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
