    <node id="Microsatellite Instability">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Instability</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite Instability is a genetic feature observed in some cancers that can influence treatment decisions. It refers to errors in DNA replication that lead to changes in microsatellite sequences, which are short, repeated DNA segments. Microsatellite instability (MSI) is a type of biomarker found in some colon cancers, indicating genetic instability. It is also described as the presence of abnormal DNA sequences due to defects in mismatch repair proteins, which are responsible for correcting errors during DNA replication.  

Microsatellite instability is a type of genetic mutation associated with certain cancers, particularly colorectal cancer. It is linked to defective mismatch repair mechanisms, leading to changes in the length of microsatellites. This condition is considered a genetic marker associated with specific types of cancer and can indicate response to certain therapies. Additionally, it is a genetic feature that can influence treatment decisions, as its presence may guide the selection of targeted treatments.  

Overall, Microsatellite Instability (MSI) is a key indicator of genetic instability in cancer cells, often related to impaired DNA repair processes. Its detection plays an important role in both diagnosis and therapeutic planning for patients with certain malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
