    <node id="Claustrophobia">
      <data key="d0">Claustrophobia</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Claustrophobia is a fear of enclosed spaces that can significantly affect a patient's experience during medical procedures such as MRI scans. This condition involves a sense of dread or anxiety when in confined areas, which may influence a patient's choice between closed and open MRI machines. Claustrophobia is often described as an intense fear or anxiety of being in enclosed spaces, which can make undergoing certain medical procedures, like MRI scans, difficult. The fear may lead to discomfort during a closed MRI and can impact the overall experience of the patient. It is a common concern among individuals undergoing imaging procedures, as the confined environment of the MRI machine can trigger distress. Multiple descriptions highlight the connection between claustrophobia and the challenges associated with MRI scans, emphasizing its potential to hinder the process. The condition is consistently defined as a fear of enclosed spaces, with variations in how it affects patients' experiences and choices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
