      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic breast cancer (MBC) is breast cancer that has spread outside the breast and lymph nodes under the arm (axilla).&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic breast cancer is cancer that has spread from the breast to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic breast cancer (MBC) is cancer that has spread beyond the breast and lymph nodes to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic breast cancer is cancer that has spread beyond the breast to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic breast cancer (MBC) is cancer that has spread beyond the breast to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptor Status">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptor Status</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone receptor status refers to the presence of receptors for hormones such as estrogen or progesterone in breast cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone receptor status refers to whether breast cancer cells have receptors for estrogen or progesterone.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone receptor status refers to the presence of estrogen and progesterone receptors in breast cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone receptor status refers to the presence of receptors for hormones like estrogen or progesterone in breast cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839691</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 Status">
      <data key="d0">HER2 Status</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 status indicates whether breast cancer cells have too much of the HER2 protein, which can affect treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 status indicates whether breast cancer cells have excessive amounts of the HER2 protein.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839692</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Cancer Risk Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Cancer Risk Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic cancer risk testing is a method used to determine a person's likelihood of developing cancer based on their genetic profile.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing analyzes DNA to identify inherited mutations that may increase cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing is a method used to identify genetic mutations that may increase cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing identifies inherited genetic mutations that may increase the risk of developing cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing involves analyzing DNA to identify genetic mutations that may increase the risk of developing cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Cancer Risk Testing analyzes blood or saliva samples to detect inherited gene mutations associated with cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing identifies inherited genetic mutations that may increase cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839692</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Primary Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary tumor is a mass of abnormal cells that forms at the site where cancer originates.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary tumor is the initial mass of cancer cells that forms at the site of origin.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary tumor is a mass formed by cancer cells that originate in the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839692</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph vessels are part of the lymphatic system and can carry cancer cells to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph vessels are part of the lymphatic system and transport lymph throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph vessels are tubes that carry lymph throughout the body and connect to lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph vessels are part of the lymphatic system, carrying lymph throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph vessels are part of the lymphatic system and can be blocked by cancer cells in inflammatory breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph vessels are part of the lymphatic system and transport lymph fluid through the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839692</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">symptom</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms are physical or mental indications of a disease or condition, such as weight gain, muscle weakness, hormonal changes, headaches, seizures, mood changes, and other observable or reported changes. They serve as indicators of a medical condition and can be physical, mental, or psychological in nature. Symptoms are often reported by the patient and are used to detect various illnesses, including ovarian cancer, where symptoms may include bloating, heartburn, pelvic pain, and frequent urination. Additionally, symptoms play a crucial role in guiding treatment decisions, as they help healthcare professionals identify and manage underlying conditions. While some descriptions emphasize the physical aspects of symptoms, others highlight their mental or psychological components, reflecting the broad range of ways in which diseases manifest. Overall, symptoms provide essential clues for diagnosing and understanding health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839692</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Axillary lymph nodes are located near the armpit and are common sites for breast cancer to spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are located near the armpit and are common sites for breast cancer metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are located near the armpit and are part of the lymphatic system.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are located in the armpit area and can be affected by breast cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located near the armpit that drain lymph from the breast.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are located in the armpit and are part of the lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
