    </node>
    <node id="PDEMA/Mohs Surgery">
      <data key="d0">PDEMA/Mohs Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">PDEMA/Mohs surgery is a specialized technique for removing skin cancer with high precision and minimal damage to surrounding tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839697</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d0">Standard Surgical Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard surgical excision involves removing the tumor along with a margin of normal skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard surgical excision is a procedure where cancerous tissue and a margin of healthy tissue are removed.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard surgical excision is a treatment method for high-risk or very high-risk local CSCC involving removal of a wider margin of normal tissue around the lesion.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard surgical excision is a procedure to remove cancerous tissue along with a margin of healthy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839697</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Actinic Keratoses">
      <data key="d0">Actinic Keratoses</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Actinic keratoses are rough or scaly areas of skin caused by long-term sun exposure and can develop into squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinic keratoses are pre-cancerous lesions that can develop into squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinic keratoses are pre-cancerous skin lesions caused by prolonged sun exposure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839697</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">condition</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell skin cancer is the second most common type of skin cancer, often treatable and curable.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer that can be treated with various therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer that can develop in old wounds, burns, or scars, and is referred to as Marjolin’s ulcer when it occurs in such areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the squamous cells of the skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the squamous cells of the skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839697</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Local Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Local Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">condition</data>
      <data key="d2">Local cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma refers to skin cancer that has not spread beyond its original location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
