    <node id="High-Risk or Very High-Risk">
      <data key="d0">High-Risk or Very High-Risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-risk or very high-risk refers to cancers with a higher likelihood of recurrence or spreading to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839697</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Location">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Location</data>
      <data key="d1">attribute</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor location refers to where the cancer is situated on the body, which can influence risk levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor location refers to the specific area within the body where a tumor is found.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839697</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Size">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Size</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor size refers to the dimensions of the cancerous growth, which can impact risk classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor size refers to the dimensions of a cancerous growth and is an important factor in cancer staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor size is a measurement used to assess the extent and severity of a cancerous growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor size is a measure of the dimensions of a tumor, often used in cancer staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor size refers to the measurement of the primary tumor in breast cancer, typically expressed in centimeters or millimeters.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor size is measured in centimeters (cm) to determine staging, with specific ranges for each category.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor size refers to the physical dimensions of a cancerous growth, often used to assess treatment effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839697</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Shape">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Shape</data>
      <data key="d1">attribute</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor shape refers to the appearance and boundaries of the cancer, which may indicate risk level.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839697</data>
