    <node id="Deletion">
      <data key="d0">Deletion</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Deletion is a type of genetic change where part of a chromosome or gene is missing, such as del(5q).&lt;SEP&gt;Deletion is a type of genetic change where part of a chromosome or gene is missing.&lt;SEP&gt;Deletion is a genetic change where a portion of a chromosome or gene is missing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839699</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Insertion">
      <data key="d0">Insertion</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Insertion is a genetic change where a new part of a chromosome or gene is included.&lt;SEP&gt;Insertion is a type of genetic change where a new part of a chromosome or gene is added.&lt;SEP&gt;Insertion is a genetic change where a new segment is added to a chromosome or gene.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839699</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Inversion">
      <data key="d0">Inversion</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Inversion is a genetic change where parts within a chromosome are switched, such as inv(16) and inv(3).&lt;SEP&gt;Inversion is a type of genetic change where parts of a chromosome are switched in position.&lt;SEP&gt;Inversion is a genetic change where a segment of a chromosome is reversed in position.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Point Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Point Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A point mutation is a genetic change where part of a gene is altered.&lt;SEP&gt;A point mutation is a type of genetic change where a single nucleotide in a gene is altered.&lt;SEP&gt;A point mutation is a genetic change where a single nucleotide in a gene is altered.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome Translocation">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome Translocation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosome translocation is a genetic change where parts between two chromosomes are switched.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromosome translocation is a genetic change where parts of chromosomes switch places.&lt;SEP&gt;A chromosome translocation is a genetic change where parts of two chromosomes are exchanged.&lt;SEP&gt;A chromosome translocation is a genetic change where parts of two chromosomes are swapped.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Gene Rearrangement">
      <data key="d0">Gene Rearrangement</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene rearrangement is a genetic change where parts of genes are switched, often at the gene level.&lt;SEP&gt;A gene rearrangement is a genetic change where parts of genes are reorganized.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Insurance">
      <data key="d0">Insurance</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Insurance is a financial service that covers medical costs, including cancer treatment and related expenses. It refers to the financial protection provided by an insurance company to cover medical expenses, including treatment costs. Insurance is a financial product that provides protection against potential losses, including medical expenses. It also includes coverage for medical treatments and services through health insurance plans. Some descriptions highlight that insurance may influence access to cancer care, as patients may still face copays and uncovered expenses even with coverage. Additionally, insurance is sometimes described as an organization that provides coverage for medical costs, though this definition is less precise than the financial service perspective. Insurance is a financial service that covers medical costs, including standard cancer care during clinical trials, and it plays a critical role in mitigating the financial burden of healthcare for individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Marrow Biopsy is a medical procedure used to collect samples of bone marrow for testing. The procedure typically involves removing a small sample of bone marrow, which can be either liquid or solid, depending on the method used. A bone marrow biopsy may involve taking a small piece of bone along with the marrow, or it may focus solely on extracting the spongy tissue found inside bones. The primary purpose of the procedure is to examine the bone marrow for abnormalities, such as cancer cells, infections, or other diseases. It is commonly performed by inserting a needle into the hip bone to obtain a core sample of bone marrow. The collected samples are then analyzed in a laboratory to help diagnose various conditions and guide treatment decisions. The procedure is generally carried out under local anesthesia, and patients may experience some discomfort during or after the process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspirate">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspirate</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Marrow Aspirate is a medical procedure used to collect bone marrow samples for testing and analysis. The procedure involves removing a small amount of liquid bone marrow, typically from the hip bone, for diagnostic purposes. A bone marrow aspirate is commonly performed to examine the composition of bone marrow, which can help in diagnosing various blood disorders, cancers, and other conditions. The process entails drawing liquid bone marrow from the bone, often through a needle, and is used to obtain samples for laboratory analysis. This procedure is essential in evaluating the health of the bone marrow and its ability to produce blood cells. It is also referred to as a method of collecting bone marrow for testing, with the primary goal of analyzing the cellular components within the sample. The exact technique may vary slightly depending on the clinical context, but the core purpose remains the same: to retrieve a representative sample for further examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RUNX1::RUNX1T">
      <data key="d0">RUNX1::RUNX1T</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">RUNX1::RUNX1T is a gene rearrangement example, often associated with specific leukemia subtypes.&lt;SEP&gt;RUNX1::RUNX1T is a genetic fusion associated with leukemia predisposition syndromes.&lt;SEP&gt;RUNX1::RUNX1T is a specific gene rearrangement associated with certain subtypes of leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nervous System Damage">
      <data key="d0">Nervous System Damage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nervous system damage refers to potential harm caused by radiation therapy, including long-term cognitive issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Nervous system damage is a risk associated with radiation therapy, particularly in older adults, and may lead to long-term cognitive issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutation is a change in DNA that can lead to genetic disorders or cancer. A mutation is a change in the genetic code that can lead to changes in proteins or cellular function. It is also described as an error in the genetic code that can result in disease. Mutation can refer to an abnormal change in a gene that may lead to disease, including cancer, or a change in the DNA sequence that can lead to the development of cancer or other diseases. Some descriptions emphasize that a mutation can alter the function of a gene, potentially leading to disease if the gene is unable to produce a normal protein. Mutations are often associated with cancer development and can occur spontaneously. They are also considered a change in DNA that can lead to abnormal cell function. Overall, mutations are changes in the genetic material that have the potential to disrupt normal biological processes and contribute to various health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839700</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Rituximab">
      <data key="d0">Rituximab</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Rituximab is a monoclonal antibody immunotherapy drug used to treat primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is an immunotherapy drug often used in combination with chemotherapy for treating Primary CNS Lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is a medication used in immunotherapy to treat certain types of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is a medication that may be added to chemotherapy for MGZL, enhancing its effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is a medication that binds to CD20 on B cells and is used in the treatment of certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CD20 Proteins">
      <data key="d0">CD20 Proteins</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">CD20 proteins are specific surface markers on cancerous blood cells that Rituximab targets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cell Rescue">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cell Rescue</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Stem cell rescue is a procedure that restores blood cells after high-dose chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue is a medical procedure used to restore blood cells after high-dose chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue is a procedure that regrows bone marrow by reintroducing harvested stem cells after high-dose chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem Cell Rescue is a medical procedure involving the removal, preservation, and reinfusion of stem cells to regenerate blood-forming cells after high-dose chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopoietic stem cells are another term for blood stem cells, responsible for generating all types of blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematopoietic stem cells are basic cells that can develop into various blood cells and are used in stem cell rescue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-Dose Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">High-Dose Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">High-dose chemotherapy is an intensive treatment used to destroy cancer cells and is followed by stem cell rescue.&lt;SEP&gt;High-Dose Chemotherapy is a treatment that destroys cancer cells but also eliminates stem cells in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839701</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vision Problems">
      <data key="d0">Vision Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Vision problems are potential side effects of radiation therapy targeting the eyes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
