    <node id="Risk Factors">
      <data key="d0">Risk Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk Factors are elements that increase the likelihood of developing various diseases. These factors can include hereditary conditions, as well as specific characteristics or behaviors that contribute to the development of different types of cancer. For example, risk factors for basal cell skin cancer include certain genetic traits and environmental exposures. Similarly, risk factors for squamous cell skin cancer involve elements that heighten the probability of this type of cancer occurring. In the case of lung cancer, risk factors commonly include smoking and exposure to second-hand smoke. For pancreatic cancer, risk factors may consist of conditions such as diabetes, smoking, and family history. Additionally, risk factors for prostate cancer are defined as conditions or characteristics that raise the chances of its development. Risk factors also apply to cervical cancer, where specific elements increase the likelihood of this disease occurring. Overall, risk factors vary depending on the disease in question but generally refer to any element that influences the probability of illness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
