    <node id="Local CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Local CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Local squamous cell skin cancer refers to cancer that has not spread to nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Local Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that is confined to the area of origin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Regional CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Regional CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Regional squamous cell skin cancer refers to cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Regional Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes or tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839729</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Nausea">
      <data key="d0">Nausea</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Nausea is a common side effect of chemotherapy, often accompanied by vomiting and other gastrointestinal symptoms. It is characterized as a feeling of sickness in the stomach that may lead to vomiting. Nausea can also be a common side effect of systemic therapy, and it is frequently observed in patients undergoing treatment for various cancers, including anal cancer and primary CNS lymphoma. In some cases, nausea is associated with brain tumors or other conditions affecting the central nervous system. The occurrence of nausea during chemotherapy is often linked to the death of fast-growing cells, and it can be managed with appropriate care and medication. Additionally, nausea may arise as a short-term side effect of radiation therapy. Despite being a well-documented phenomenon, the exact mechanisms behind chemotherapy-induced nausea remain complex and vary among individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vomiting">
      <data key="d0">Vomiting</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Vomiting is the forceful expulsion of stomach contents through the mouth, often caused by cancer treatment. It is a common side effect of chemotherapy, typically associated with nausea and other gastrointestinal issues. Vomiting can also occur due to pressure on the central nervous system from a lymphoma tumor. This condition is frequently observed in patients undergoing chemotherapy and is linked to damage to healthy cells. Additionally, vomiting is a possible side effect of systemic therapy and may be managed with appropriate care. In some cases, it is specifically noted as a common side effect of chemotherapy regimens used in the treatment of anal cancer. Vomiting is often addressed through supportive care measures designed to alleviate its impact on patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fatigue">
      <data key="d0">Fatigue</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Fatigue is a common side effect of cancer treatment, characterized by extreme tiredness. It is frequently associated with chemotherapy and radiation therapy, often persisting for weeks after treatment has ended. Fatigue can also be a side effect of systemic therapy, which includes various forms of cancer treatment administered throughout the body. In addition to being a result of treatment, fatigue may also be a symptom of certain medical conditions, such as gliomas, where it is described as debilitating and unrelated to sleep or activity levels. Fatigue is not limited to physical causes; it can also have cognitive or emotional origins. While some descriptions refer to it as a short-term effect, others highlight its prolonged nature. Overall, fatigue is a frequent symptom experienced by patients undergoing cancer treatment and is commonly described as a feeling of extreme tiredness that may be caused by treatment or other underlying factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1 Gene">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1 Gene</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The BCR::ABL1 gene is a fused gene resulting from the exchange of genetic material between chromosomes 9 and 22, linked to CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blasts">
      <data key="d0">Blasts</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Blasts are immature blood cells, often associated with leukemia and other blood disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts are immature white blood cells that are found in high numbers in AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts are immature blood cells that are abnormal in AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts are immature white blood cells that, when abnormal, can lead to the development of leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts are immature cells that can sometimes be detected in the blood, often associated with leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts are immature cells that can indicate the presence of AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts are very immature or most immature blood cells, such as myeloblasts or lymphoblasts, commonly found in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Monocytes">
      <data key="d0">Monocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Monocytes are a type of white blood cell that develop into macrophages and dendritic cells, playing a role in the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloblasts">
      <data key="d0">Myeloblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloblasts are immature blood cells that can become various types of myeloid cells and are associated with CML.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature myeloid cells that develop into mature blood cells such as granulocytes and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that can become granulocytes, red blood cells, or platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that develop into granulocytes, and their abnormal growth is characteristic of AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that are typically found in the bone marrow and are associated with acute myeloid leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Infection">
      <data key="d0">Infection</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Infection is the invasion and multiplication of microorganisms in the body. It is a condition caused by the invasion of pathogens into the body, which the immune system works to combat. Infection can also refer to a risk due to chemotherapy's suppression of the immune system, as well as a possible complication following a biopsy, characterized by the presence of harmful microorganisms in the body. Infection is an unwanted complication that can occur after a biopsy, caused by bacteria entering the body. Additionally, it is an illness caused by harmful microorganisms, which can occur as a side effect of cancer treatment. Infection may also appear as bright spots on PET scans, indicating areas of active microbial activity. Infection is a risk associated with intraocular chemotherapy, necessitating prompt treatment. An infection is a condition caused by the invasion and multiplication of microorganisms in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Blast Phase CML">
      <data key="d0">Blast Phase CML</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Blast phase CML is an advanced form of CML where there is an increased number of blast cells, either lymphoid or myeloid.&lt;SEP&gt;Blast Phase CML (BP-CML) is an advanced form of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia characterized by a high number of blasts in the blood or bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Blast phase CML (BP-CML) is an advanced form of CML where there is an increased number of lymphoid or myeloid blasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Results">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Results</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging results are visual data obtained from medical imaging procedures like CT scans, MRI, and PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging results are visual data obtained from medical scans such as CT or MRI.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging results are findings obtained from medical imaging procedures such as mammograms, MRIs, and ultrasounds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839730</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors is a medical resource providing guidance on the diagnosis and treatment of adrenal tumors.</data>
