    <node id="Body">
      <data key="d0">Body</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The body is the physical structure of a living organism.&lt;SEP&gt;The body is the main part of the stomach where food is mixed with digestive juices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Antrum">
      <data key="d0">Antrum</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The antrum is the lower portion of the stomach where food mixes with enzymes and acid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pylorus">
      <data key="d0">Pylorus</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The pylorus is a valve that controls the movement of chyme from the stomach to the small intestine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagogastric Junction">
      <data key="d0">Esophagogastric Junction</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The esophagogastric junction is where the esophagus meets the stomach, also known as the gastroesophageal junction.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagogastric junction is the point where the esophagus connects to the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mucosa">
      <data key="d0">Mucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Mucosa is the innermost layer of the esophagus, in direct contact with food.&lt;SEP&gt;The innermost layer of the esophagus wall, where cancer typically begins.&lt;SEP&gt;Mucosa is the innermost layer of the colon wall that comes into contact with food.&lt;SEP&gt;The mucosa is the innermost layer of the stomach wall, where cancer often begins.&lt;SEP&gt;The mucosa is the innermost layer of the stomach wall that comes into contact with food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Submucosa">
      <data key="d0">Submucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Submucosa is a layer of connective tissue containing blood vessels, nerves, and lymphatic vessels.&lt;SEP&gt;The submucosa is a layer of the esophagus wall beneath the mucosa, containing blood vessels and nerves.&lt;SEP&gt;Submucosa is the second layer of the colon wall, containing blood vessels, nerves, and glands.&lt;SEP&gt;The submucosa is the second layer of the stomach wall, which can be invaded by advanced stomach cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The submucosa is a layer of connective tissue beneath the mucosa that contains blood vessels and nerves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Subserosa">
      <data key="d0">Subserosa</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The subserosa is a layer of the stomach wall that may be involved in advanced stages of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The subserosa is a layer of connective tissue that supports the serosa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839736</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Serosa">
      <data key="d0">Serosa</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The serosa is a thin membrane that covers organs and is also known as the serous membrane or visceral peritoneum.&lt;SEP&gt;Serosa is an outer membrane covering the gallbladder and bile ducts.&lt;SEP&gt;Serosa is the outermost layer of the colon wall, also known as adventitia.&lt;SEP&gt;The serosa is the outermost layer of the stomach wall, which can be affected as cancer progresses.&lt;SEP&gt;The serosa is the outer membrane of the stomach that allows it to move smoothly against other organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839737</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Intestinal Type Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Intestinal Type Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The intestinal type of adenocarcinoma forms tube- and gland-shaped structures and may respond to targeted therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839737</data>
