    </node>
    <node id="Calcium">
      <data key="d0">Calcium</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Calcium is a mineral that plays a key role in bone health and may be supplemented in thyroid cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium is a mineral necessary for healthy teeth, bones, and tissues, and its levels are checked in a comprehensive metabolic panel.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium is an essential mineral involved in bone health and function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839750</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hypercalcemia">
      <data key="d0">Hypercalcemia</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hypercalcemia is a condition characterized by high levels of calcium in the blood, often caused by bone damage from cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839750</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lactate Dehydrogenase">
      <data key="d0">Lactate Dehydrogenase</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Lactate dehydrogenase is an enzyme in the blood that can indicate tissue damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) or lactic acid dehydrogenase is an enzyme found in most cells. Dying cells release LDH into blood. Fast-growing cells, such as tumor cells, also release LDH.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme found in most cells that is released when cells die.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme that can be elevated in AML patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme involved in energy production, and elevated levels may indicate cell damage caused by cancer or other conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme found in most cells, with elevated levels indicating cell damage or rapid cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839750</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="X-ray">
      <data key="d0">X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">An x-ray is a type of radiation used to create images of the inside of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839750</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Damage">
      <data key="d0">Bone Damage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone damage refers to any abnormal condition affecting the structure or integrity of bones.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone damage refers to the deterioration or injury of bone tissue, which can be caused by cancer, treatment, or other health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone damage refers to any injury or deterioration of bone tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone damage refers to the deterioration or injury of bone tissue, often caused by cancer or treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
