    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal cell skin cancer is a type of cancer that can recur in the original area or spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal cell skin cancer is the most common type of skin cancer, often curable when detected early. It arises from abnormal growth of basal cells in the epidermis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839757</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) is another name for basal cell skin cancer, which is the most common form of skin cancer and typically treatable.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal cell carcinoma is the most common type of skin cancer, often referred to as a nonmelanoma skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839757</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="United States">
      <data key="d0">United States</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The United States is a country where approximately 3 million cases of basal cell skin cancer are diagnosed annually.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hypodermis">
      <data key="d0">Hypodermis</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">The hypodermis is the deepest layer of the skin, made up of fat and connective tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypodermis is the deepest layer of the skin, composed of fat and connective tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cells">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal cells are found in the lower part of the epidermis and continuously divide to replace squamous cells on the skin's surface.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal cells are found in the lower part of the epidermis and continuously divide to replace squamous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cells">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cells are flat cells located in the upper part of the epidermis and are involved in forming the skin's outer layer.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cells are flat, scale-like cells found in the outer layer of the skin and other body areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cells are thin, flat cells that form the surface lining of the nasopharynx and are the origin of most nasopharyngeal carcinomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Melanocytes">
      <data key="d0">Melanocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Melanocytes are cells in the lower part of the epidermis that produce melanin, which determines skin, hair, and eye color.&lt;SEP&gt;Melanocytes are cells that produce melanin, responsible for skin, hair, and eye color, and protect against UV damage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="UV Radiation">
      <data key="d0">UV Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">UV radiation from the sun or tanning beds can damage DNA in skin cells, leading to the development of basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Suppression">
      <data key="d0">Immune Suppression</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune suppression increases the risk of developing basal cell skin cancer, particularly in individuals with weakened immune systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Organ Transplants">
      <data key="d0">Organ Transplants</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Organ transplants can lead to immune suppression, increasing the risk of basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Autoimmune Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Autoimmune Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Autoimmune diseases can weaken the immune system, making individuals more susceptible to basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tanning Beds">
      <data key="d0">Tanning Beds</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Tanning beds are artificial sources of UV radiation that increase the risk of developing basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Melanin">
      <data key="d0">Melanin</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Melanin is a pigment produced by melanocytes that protects the skin from harmful ultraviolet rays.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Distant Parts of the Body">
      <data key="d0">Distant Parts of the Body</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Distant parts of the body refer to areas beyond the original site of basal cell skin cancer, where the cancer might spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prevention">
      <data key="d0">Prevention</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Prevention involves measures to reduce the risk of developing or recurring basal cell skin cancer, such as sun protection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Exams">
      <data key="d0">Skin Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin exams are regular check-ups performed by doctors to monitor for signs of basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fair Skin">
      <data key="d0">Fair Skin</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Fair skin is a risk factor for basal cell skin cancer, as it offers less natural protection against UV radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Light Eye Color">
      <data key="d0">Light Eye Color</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Light eye color is a risk factor for basal cell skin cancer, associated with increased sensitivity to UV radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Surveillance refers to the regular monitoring of patients after treatment to detect any signs of cancer recurrence. It is a strategy that involves ongoing observation and assessment without immediate further intervention, focusing on identifying early indications of disease return. Surveillance is commonly used in cancer care to track patients following successful treatment, ensuring timely detection of any potential relapse. This process may include various methods such as imaging, physical exams, and laboratory tests, depending on the type of cancer and individual patient circumstances. Surveillance is specifically applied in different contexts, including cervical cancer and anal cancer, where it aims to identify recurrence at an early stage. The primary goal of surveillance is to monitor for signs of cancer recurrence after treatment, allowing for prompt action if needed. It is also described as a monitoring strategy for cancer that has been treated with no immediate further intervention. Overall, surveillance serves as a critical component in post-treatment care, emphasizing continuous evaluation to ensure patient health and safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Red or Blond Hair">
      <data key="d0">Red or Blond Hair</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Red or blond hair is a risk factor for basal cell skin cancer, linked to lower levels of melanin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="History of Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">History of Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A history of skin cancer increases the likelihood of developing basal cell skin cancer in the future.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation treatment for other medical conditions can increase the risk of developing basal cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation treatment is a common initial therapy for anal cancer that can weaken the perineum and affect post-surgery healing.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical treatment used to target cancer, which can weaken bones in the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Types of Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Types of Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Types of treatment for basal cell skin cancer include surgery, radiation therapy, and systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 2">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 2</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 2 discusses the testing methods used to diagnose basal cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 2 provides an overview of tests related to ALL, including fertility impact, genetic and biomarker testing, and diagnostic procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 2 covers testing for bone cancer, including fertility impacts and biomarker testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 3">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 3</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 3 provides information on the types of treatment for cancer and how to make decisions with your care team.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 3 covers the different types of treatment available for basal cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 3 outlines the general treatment approach for ALL, including steroids, chemotherapy, and other systemic therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 3 explains the staging of bone cancer and where tumors may be found in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 4">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 4</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 4 discusses treatment options based on risk and recurrence of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 4 provides information on treatment options based on the risk and recurrence of basal cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 4 discusses supportive care and potential side effects associated with ALL treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Chapter 4 outlines types of treatment for bone cancer and the roles of different members of the treatment team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urination Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Urination Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Urination symptoms include frequent urination, especially at night, weak or intermittent urine stream, and difficulty holding in urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Semen">
      <data key="d0">Semen</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">Semen is a fluid composed of liquids from the prostate and seminal vesicles, as well as sperm from the testicles.&lt;SEP&gt;Semen is a fluid containing sperm and secretions from the prostate and seminal vesicles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Factors">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic factors, such as mutations in the BRCA2 gene, are linked to an increased risk of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839759</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Diet">
      <data key="d0">Diet</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Diet, particularly high-fat diets, is associated with an increased risk of prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet refers to food choices that may influence the risk of developing prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lifestyle">
      <data key="d0">Lifestyle</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Lifestyle factors such as exercise, weight loss, smoking, and obesity influence the risk and aggressiveness of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Early-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage prostate cancer has not spread beyond the prostate and is often curable.&lt;SEP&gt;Early-stage prostate cancer is highly treatable and often curable, with a high survival rate when diagnosed early.&lt;SEP&gt;Early-stage prostate cancer is a classification of cancer that has not spread beyond the prostate.&lt;SEP&gt;Early-Stage Prostate Cancer has not visibly spread beyond the prostate and usually grows slowly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Regional Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Regional Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Regional prostate cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes but not farther.&lt;SEP&gt;Regional prostate cancer is a classification of cancer that has spread to nearby tissues or lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Regional Prostate Cancer refers to cancer that has spread to nearby areas such as lymph nodes but not farther.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic prostate cancer has spread to distant organs such as the bones, liver, or lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic prostate cancer is advanced-stage prostate cancer that has spread to other areas of the body such as lymph nodes, bones, or organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic prostate cancer is a classification of cancer that has spread beyond the prostate to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic Prostate Cancer refers to cancer that has spread to distant parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Tests">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic tests are used to confirm the presence of prostate cancer and determine its stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic tests are procedures used to confirm cancer, determine its stage, and monitor treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic tests are procedures used to identify and confirm the presence of diseases, such as breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic Tests are medical procedures used to identify and understand health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA-PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">PSMA-PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A PSMA-PET scan is a specialized PET scan that targets prostate-specific membrane antigen on cancer cells for better detection.&lt;SEP&gt;PSMA-PET scan is a diagnostic imaging technique that uses tracers targeting PSMA to detect and monitor prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen">
      <data key="d0">Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA) is a protein found on the surface of prostate cancer cells, used as a target for imaging tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA) is a protein found on the surface of prostate cancer cells, used as a target for specific imaging techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen (PSMA) is a protein found on the surface of prostate cancer cells, which is used as a target for diagnostic and therapeutic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Underarms">
      <data key="d0">Underarms</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Underarms are the areas under the arms where lymph nodes may be found and examined for enlargement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neck">
      <data key="d0">Neck</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Neck is the part of the body connecting the head to the torso, and is included in a head and neck exam.&lt;SEP&gt;Neck is a body region where lymph nodes may be palpated and checked for signs of disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CT">
      <data key="d0">CT</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">CT is a medical imaging technique used to determine the location of cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;CT is a type of imaging test that uses X-rays to create cross-sectional images of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;CT refers to computed tomography, a type of imaging test that uses x-rays to produce detailed cross-sectional images of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;CT stands for Computed Tomography.&lt;SEP&gt;CT is an imaging technique used to design the brachytherapy treatment plan.&lt;SEP&gt;CT is a medical imaging technique that uses X-rays to produce detailed cross-sectional images of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PET">
      <data key="d0">PET</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">PET is a type of imaging test that uses radioactive tracers to detect metabolic activity in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;PET refers to positron emission tomography, a type of imaging test that uses radioactive tracers to detect cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA">
      <data key="d0">PSMA</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">PSMA is a protein found on the surface of prostate cancer cells, used as a target for specific imaging techniques like PSMA-PET scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Panoramic Dental X-Ray">
      <data key="d0">Panoramic Dental X-Ray</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A panoramic dental x-ray is an imaging technique that captures a wide view of the mouth and surrounding structures.&lt;SEP&gt;A panoramic dental x-ray captures the entire mouth in one image, used for planning dental treatments such as dentures and implants.&lt;SEP&gt;Panoramic dental x-ray is a type of dental imaging that captures a wide view of the mouth, including teeth, jaws, and surrounding structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck cancers refer to a group of cancers affecting the head and neck region, including the mouth, throat, and lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers are a group of cancers that occur in the head or neck area, including the mouth, throat, and larynx.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers refer to a group of cancers that develop in the head and neck region, including the throat.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers are a group of cancers that occur in the head or neck area of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers are a group of cancers arising in the head or neck area, including the nasopharynx, larynx, and oropharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers are a group of malignancies that occur in the head and neck region, including the mouth, throat, and larynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839760</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Histology">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Histology</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor histology refers to the microscopic structure of a tumor, used to determine its type and characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor histology refers to the classification of cancer based on the appearance of its cells under a microscope.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor histology refers to the microscopic examination of tissue samples to determine the specific type and characteristics of a tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839761</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Fine-Needle Biopsy (FNB) is a method of collecting tissue samples using a needle.&lt;SEP&gt;A fine-needle biopsy is a procedure where a thin needle is used to extract a small sample of tissue for diagnostic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-needle biopsy is a procedure that uses a thin needle to collect a small sample of tissue for examination to detect diseases such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839761</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Core Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Core Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Core needle biopsy is another type of biopsy used to evaluate lymph nodes for CSCC spread.&lt;SEP&gt;A core needle biopsy uses a wide needle to remove a tissue sample from a lymph node without removing the entire node.&lt;SEP&gt;Core needle biopsy is a procedure that uses a larger needle to obtain a core of tissue for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Core Needle Biopsy (CNB) is a biopsy technique that removes a larger tissue sample using a needle.&lt;SEP&gt;Core needle biopsy is a medical procedure that involves using a larger needle to remove a cylindrical sample of tissue for analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A Core Needle Biopsy is a procedure that removes tissue samples using a hollow needle, often performed under light sedation.&lt;SEP&gt;A core needle biopsy is a procedure used to collect tissue samples for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839761</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A lymph node biopsy is performed if there is suspicion that cancer has spread to the lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;A lymph node biopsy is a procedure to examine lymph nodes for signs of disease.&lt;SEP&gt;A lymph node biopsy involves removing a sample of lymph node tissue for examination to detect cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node biopsy is a procedure where a sample of tissue from a lymph node is taken for examination to detect diseases such as cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A lymph node biopsy is a procedure that removes a sample of lymph node tissue for testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839761</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Radio Waves">
      <data key="d0">Radio Waves</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Radio waves are a type of electromagnetic wave used in MRI scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Radio waves are used in MRI to generate images of the body's internal structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Radio waves are a type of electromagnetic radiation used in MRI scans to generate detailed images of the body's internal structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839761</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Field">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Field</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A magnetic field is generated by the magnet in MRI machines to align the protons in the body for imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;A magnetic field is a force field created by magnets and is essential for the operation of MRI scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839761</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fluorodeoxyglucose">
      <data key="d0">Fluorodeoxyglucose</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Fluorodeoxyglucose is a radiotracer used in FDG-PET/CT scans to detect areas of increased metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorodeoxyglucose is a radiotracer used in FDG-PET/CT scans to detect cancerous cells based on their metabolic activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839761</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Glucose">
      <data key="d0">Glucose</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Glucose is a simple sugar used in the production of FDG-PET/CT tracers.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is a simple sugar that is used by the body for energy and is a component of the radiotracer fluorodeoxyglucose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839761</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fluoride">
      <data key="d0">Fluoride</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Fluoride is a chemical element used in the production of FDG-PET/CT tracers.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride is a chemical compound used in the production of the radiotracer fluorodeoxyglucose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839761</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Allergic Reaction">
      <data key="d0">Allergic Reaction</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">An allergic reaction is an immune system response to substances like contrast material.&lt;SEP&gt;An allergic reaction is an immune system response to a substance that is typically harmless to most people.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839761</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Urine">
      <data key="d0">Urine</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Urine is the bodily fluid through which contrast material is excreted after a medical imaging procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;Urine is a sample used for genetic germline testing to identify inherited mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Urine is a liquid waste product produced by the kidneys and excreted from the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839761</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metal">
      <data key="d0">Metal</data>
      <data key="d1">artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Metal in the body can interfere with MRI scans due to the strong magnetic field.&lt;SEP&gt;Metal refers to any metallic objects in the body that may interfere with MRI scans due to the strong magnetic fields.&lt;SEP&gt;Metal is a material that can be attracted by magnets and may interfere with MRI scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
