    <node id="Iodine">
      <data key="d0">Iodine</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Iodine is a chemical element used in some contrast materials and can cause allergic reactions in some individuals.&lt;SEP&gt;Iodine is a mineral necessary for the production of thyroid hormones and is found in certain foods and iodized salt.&lt;SEP&gt;Iodine is a chemical element that cancer cells may or may not absorb, influencing treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839805</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Primary Bone Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary bone cancer originates in the bone or cartilage and is distinct from cancers that spread to the bone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839805</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Bone Malignancy">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Bone Malignancy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondary bone malignancy refers to cancer that spreads to the bone from another part of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839805</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomas">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomas are rare types of cancer that originate in connective tissues and are not discussed in this patient guide.&lt;SEP&gt;Sarcomas are a rare group of cancers that can be either soft tissue or bone-based.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839805</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Soft Tissue Sarcomas">
      <data key="d0">Soft Tissue Sarcomas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Soft tissue sarcomas are a type of sarcoma that develops in soft tissues such as fat, muscles, and nerves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839805</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Sarcomas">
      <data key="d0">Bone Sarcomas</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone sarcomas are a type of sarcoma that originates in the bone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839805</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Osteosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Osteosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteosarcoma is a common type of bone cancer that starts in the cells that form bone.&lt;SEP&gt;Osteosarcoma is a type of cancer that originates in the cells that form bone.&lt;SEP&gt;Osteosarcoma is a common type of bone cancer that requires a combination of therapies for treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839805</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ewing Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Ewing Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Ewing sarcoma is a type of bone cancer that begins in the bone marrow and often occurs in long bones or flat bones.&lt;SEP&gt;Ewing Sarcoma is a type of cancer characterized by a genetic fusion between the EWS gene and members of the ETS gene family.&lt;SEP&gt;Ewing sarcoma is a type of bone cancer that often begins with systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839805</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Myeloma">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Myeloma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiple myeloma is a type of cancer that affects plasma cells in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839805</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Bone Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Bone Cancer is a resource providing expert recommendations for bone cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839805</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients®: Soft Tissue Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients®: Soft Tissue Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients®: Soft Tissue Sarcoma is a resource offering guidance on treating soft tissue sarcomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839805</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients®: Multiple Myeloma">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients®: Multiple Myeloma</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients®: Multiple Myeloma is a resource providing expert recommendations for multiple myeloma care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Chordoma">
      <data key="d0">Chordoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Chordoma is a type of cancer that starts in notochordal cells and is typically found in the spine or base of the skull.&lt;SEP&gt;Chordoma is a rare type of bone cancer that may be treated with surgery or radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Giant Cell Tumor of Bone">
      <data key="d0">Giant Cell Tumor of Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Giant cell tumor of bone is an overgrowth of cells in the metaphysis of the bone.&lt;SEP&gt;Giant cell tumor of bone is not usually cancer but requires aggressive treatment to prevent damage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Compact Bone">
      <data key="d0">Compact Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Compact bone is the hard, dense outer layer of bone covered by the periosteum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Spongey Bone">
      <data key="d0">Spongey Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Spongey bone is the spongy, porous inner layer of bone containing red marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Red Marrow">
      <data key="d0">Red Marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Red marrow is found in the center of bones and contains blood stem cells that produce blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Yellow Marrow">
      <data key="d0">Yellow Marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
      <data key="d2">Yellow marrow is primarily composed of fat and replaces red marrow as a person ages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic therapies are treatments that affect the entire body and are used when other options are not available.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic thyroid cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond the thyroid gland.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="RAI">
      <data key="d0">RAI</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">RAI stands for Radioactive Iodine, a treatment used for certain types of thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic squamous cell skin cancer is a type of cancer that has spread to other areas of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Stereotactic body radiotherapy is a specialized radiation technique used to target small areas of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Removal">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Removal</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node removal is a procedure performed during surgery to remove affected lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Extracapsular Extension">
      <data key="d0">Extracapsular Extension</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Extracapsular extension refers to cancer that has grown through the protective outer layer of a lymph node.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-Up Care">
      <data key="d0">Follow-Up Care</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up care refers to the ongoing monitoring and management of cancer after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Follow-up care refers to ongoing medical check-ups and monitoring after completing cancer treatment to detect any recurrence or late effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-Risk Patients">
      <data key="d0">High-Risk Patients</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">High-risk patients are individuals who have a higher likelihood of developing or experiencing cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839806</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoma is a type of cancer that originates in the epithelial cells, specifically in the nasopharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TNM Staging">
      <data key="d0">TNM Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">TNM staging is a system used to classify the extent of cancer, including tumor size (T), lymph node involvement (N), and metastasis (M).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Tis">
      <data key="d0">Tis</data>
      <data key="d1">stage</data>
      <data key="d2">Tis represents carcinoma in situ, where cancerous cells are confined to the squamous cell layer without invasion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Parapharyngeal Space">
      <data key="d0">Parapharyngeal Space</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The parapharyngeal space is a triangular area near the pharynx containing fat, blood vessels, nerves, and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Pap Smear">
      <data key="d0">Pap Smear</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pap smear is a screening test used to detect abnormal cells in the cervix, reducing cervical cancer rates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ovary">
      <data key="d0">Ovary</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The ovary is an organ that produces eggs and hormones involved in the menstrual cycle and sexual reproduction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Fallopian Tube">
      <data key="d0">Fallopian Tube</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The fallopian tube is part of the female reproductive system and is near the ovary.&lt;SEP&gt;The fallopian tube is a structure that connects the ovary to the uterus, facilitating the movement of eggs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Ectocervix">
      <data key="d0">Ectocervix</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The ectocervix is the outer part of the cervix that extends into the vagina and is lined with squamous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The ectocervix is the outer part of the cervix that extends into the vagina.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Endocervix">
      <data key="d0">Endocervix</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The endocervix is the inner part of the cervix that forms a canal between the vagina and the uterus, lined with columnar cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The endocervix is the canal between the vagina and the body of the uterus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Squamo-Columnar Junction">
      <data key="d0">Squamo-Columnar Junction</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The squamo-columnar junction is the area where the ectocervix meets the endocervix, often referred to as the transformation zone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Stroma">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Stroma</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The cervical stroma is the thick layer of muscular tissue beneath the cervical lining.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Dysplasia">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Dysplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical dysplasia refers to abnormal cell changes on the surface of the cervix that may progress to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical dysplasia refers to abnormal cell changes on the cervix lining, often linked to HPV infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) is a term used to describe pre-cancerous changes in the cervical cells, graded based on severity.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) refers to abnormal cell changes on the cervix that may lead to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Low-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion">
      <data key="d0">Low-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (LSIL) refers to mild dysplasia (CIN 1) that may progress to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839807</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (HSIL) refers to moderate or severe dysplasia (CIN 2 and 3) that is considered pre-cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839808</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Lesion">
      <data key="d0">Lesion</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A lesion is an area of abnormal tissue, such as dysplasia or cancer, that can be found on the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839808</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="External Os">
      <data key="d0">External Os</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The external os is the opening of the cervix that allows menstrual blood to pass into the vagina and is part of the ectocervix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839808</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Internal Os">
      <data key="d0">Internal Os</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The internal os is the upper part of the endocervix that connects the cervix to the uterus and is part of the cervical canal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1772839808</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ATM">
      <data key="d0">ATM</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
