    <edge source="Pathologist" target="Biopsy">
      <data key="d7">9.0</data>
      <data key="d8">Biopsy and Pathologist refers to the collaborative process in which a pathologist examines tissue samples obtained through a biopsy to assess for the presence of cancer or other pathological conditions. A biopsy involves the removal of tissue for examination by a pathologist to determine if cancer is present. Biopsy samples are analyzed by a pathologist to confirm the presence of cancer and identify its subtype. The pathologist's role includes analyzing these samples to detect cancer cells, confirm the presence of cancer, and determine the type and characteristics of the tumor. This process is essential for diagnosing conditions such as nodules, where a pathologist evaluates biopsy samples to determine if they are cancerous. The pathologist also examines the biopsy sample to evaluate its nature and provide critical diagnostic information. Overall, the relationship between biopsy and pathologist centers on the analysis of tissue samples to guide medical diagnosis and treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d9">analysis,analysis expert,diagnosis,diagnostic analysis,diagnostic confirmation,diagnostic evaluation,diagnostic procedure,diagnostic role,medical examination,medical expertise,medical professional,medical specialist,tissue analysis,tissue evaluation</data>
      <data key="d10">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
      <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d12">1772840191</data>
      <data key="d13" />
    </edge>
